{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"At first it might seem as if we can't apply any rule we've learned to find the indefinite integral of a radical function.\n\nHowever, remember that any radical can be rewritten as a rational power.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\int (\\sqrt[3]{x})^4\\,dx=\\int x^{^{\\frac{4}{3}}}\\,dx$\n\nNow we can integrate using the reverse power rule:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int x^n\\,dx=\\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\displaystyle\\int (\\sqrt[3]{x})^4\\,dx\u0026=\\displaystyle\\int x^{^{\\frac{4}{3}}}\\,dx\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{x^{^{\\frac{4}{3}+1}}}{\\dfrac{4}{3}+1}+C\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{3}{7} x^{^{\\frac{7}{3}}}+C\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int (\\sqrt[3]{x})^4\\,dx=\\dfrac{3}{7} x^{^{\\frac{7}{3}}}+C$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\int (\\sqrt[3]{x})^4\\,dx=$ [[☃ expression 1]]$+C$","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"\\frac{3}{7}x^{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
